WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.940
Today, Africa is unfortunately known as the
poorest continent in the world by far.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:11.150
From the trillion dollar bills found in Zimbabwe
to the 85% of Africans who live on less than

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:16.270
$5.50 per day, it’s no question the Africans
have had it tough.

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:20.980
However, there are a lucky few Africans, who
have been able to accumulate extraordinary

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:22.430
amounts of wealth.

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:27.660
One of these lucky Africans is Aliko Dangote
who is currently worth $11.9 billion.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.630
So, here’s how Aliko Dangote became Africa’s
richest person.

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:38.329
Well, taking a look back, we have to go quite
a bit back in time as Aliko’s wealth literally

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:43.370
stretches back centuries starting with his
great grandfather, Alhassan Dantata.

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:47.670
Alhassan was born in 1877 in the Nigerian
town of Bebeji.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:52.520
Though he had several siblings, life wasn’t
too bad as his parents were prosperous traders

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:54.350
and caravan leaders.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:01:00.520
His father, Madugu Abdullahi, was also the
son of a successful merchant named Baba Talatin.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:03.940
And Baba Talatin was also the son of a successful
merchant.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:09.130
So, we have a long lineage of successful merchants,
but everything would turn upside down early

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:10.540
on in Alhassan’s life.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:15.670
When Alhassan was 8 years old, his father
Abdullahi would pass away and his mother would

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:18.359
leave behind the children and move to Ghana.

00:01:18.359 --> 00:01:22.759
As a result, Alhassan would find himself living
with an old slave woman named Tata.

00:01:22.759 --> 00:01:27.509
Tata raised Alhassan and his siblings and
she basically became their mother.

00:01:27.509 --> 00:01:32.299
In fact, Alhassan’s last name Dantata is
derived from the name of this woman.

00:01:32.299 --> 00:01:35.389
In the Hausa language, Dantata means son of
Tata.

00:01:35.389 --> 00:01:40.260
Though Tata was a loving woman and took care
of the children as if they were her own, as

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:42.840
you would guess, she didn’t have any money.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:46.409
So, the kids had to earn their own money to
support themselves.

00:01:46.409 --> 00:01:50.539
Some of them begged for food and clothes while
others chose to work for low wages.

00:01:50.539 --> 00:01:55.249
Alhassan was one of the few that chose to
work, but just as he started to consistently

00:01:55.249 --> 00:01:59.759
make enough money to support himself as a
teenager, the Kano civil war rolled around.

00:01:59.759 --> 00:02:03.509
Alhassan and his siblings would be captured
and sold as slaves during the civil war.

00:02:03.509 --> 00:02:07.909
Fortunately, the war didn’t last too long
and Alhassan would be able to purchase his

00:02:07.909 --> 00:02:10.470
freedom after tensions settled down.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:14.709
With basically no money left once again though,
Alhassan would travel to Ghana hoping that

00:02:14.709 --> 00:02:16.579
his biological mother could help him.

00:02:16.579 --> 00:02:21.049
To his disappointment though, his mother would
take Alhassan to a mallam, who is basically

00:02:21.049 --> 00:02:25.019
a teacher, and she told him that he can stay
with the mallam until he was ready to return

00:02:25.019 --> 00:02:26.530
to Bebeji.

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:30.900
Realizing that he was truly on his own, Alhassan
would do anything and everything possible

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:32.530
to earn a bit of money.

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:36.560
On Thursdays and Fridays, he worked for money
and during the rest of the days, he begged

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:37.810
for food.

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:40.470
Most of his earnings, though, went to his
mallam.

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:45.110
In an effort to break out of stagnation, Alhassan
would eventually move back to Bebeji and decide

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:47.769
to follow in the footsteps of his father.

00:02:47.769 --> 00:02:52.900
Starting in 1906 at an age of 29, Alhassan
began trading various European goods including

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.599
beads, necklaces, and various cloths.

00:02:55.599 --> 00:03:00.060
Alhassan conducted most of trades in the city
of Kano, but he decided to continue living

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:02.319
in Bebeji at first.

00:03:02.319 --> 00:03:05.859
Eventually though, Alhassan would relocate
to Kano.

00:03:05.859 --> 00:03:10.349
After 6 years of trading, Alhassan would finally
break out of poverty and move up to the middle

00:03:10.349 --> 00:03:11.349
class.

00:03:11.349 --> 00:03:16.969
In 1912, Europeans became interested in groundnuts,
and they contacted a bunch of well known merchants

00:03:16.969 --> 00:03:18.999
in Kano to provide them with groundnuts.

00:03:18.999 --> 00:03:24.700
At the time, Alhassan wasn’t nearly notable
enough to be contacted by the Europeans, but

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:27.740
he quickly realized the great opportunity
of exporting groundnuts.

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:32.170
You see, many merchants in Africa had become
extremely wealthy selling cocoa beans to the

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:37.609
Europeans, and Alhassan believed that he could
succeed in a similar manner using groundnuts.

00:03:37.609 --> 00:03:41.329
But first he had to get noticed by a major
European exporter.

00:03:41.329 --> 00:03:46.170
Now that Alhassan had his basic needs taken
care of, this was much easier.

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:51.680
Between 1912 and 1918, Alhassan would vigorously
build up his reputation in Kano and he would

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:53.779
save up every penny he made.

00:03:53.779 --> 00:03:56.879
Apparently, he was extremely frugal.

00:03:56.879 --> 00:04:00.650
He also worked on his English skills to make
it easier to trade with Europeans.

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:06.310
And just as he hoped, in 1918, the Royal Niger
Company owned by the British reached out to

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:08.489
Alhassan in hopes of trading groundnuts.

00:04:08.489 --> 00:04:12.860
Alhassan would instantly accept the offer
and he would quickly get to work on establishing

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:14.639
his dominance in the trade.

00:04:14.639 --> 00:04:18.780
Though Alhassan was unwilling to spend any
money on himself, he didn’t think twice

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:22.300
when it came to investing into his relationship
with the Niger Company.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:26.330
While all of his fellow merchants used pack
animals to transport the goods, Alhassan used

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:31.210
the money he saved up over the past 12 years
to ship his groundnuts on steamships.

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:35.770
This earned him a lot of respect from the
Europeans and over the next few years, many

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:38.560
of them formed partnerships with Alhassan.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:42.130
These European connections allowed Alhassan
to quickly scale up his other trading goods

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:47.090
as well including kola nuts, grains, precious
stones, and many more.

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:51.790
As his trading grew, Alhassan started to hire
employees, and some of them even lived with

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:52.790
him.

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:54.970
All of this European exposure allowed Alhassan
to quickly jump from being another successful

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:58.210
merchant to being the richest businessman
in Kano by 1922.

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:02.250
At this point, the hardest part of his journey
was behind him.

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:06.370
He had broken out of poverty, established
a name for himself, and now he was the richest

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:08.190
person in Kano.

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:11.259
All he had to do was maintain and grow his
business.

00:05:11.259 --> 00:05:14.599
And this is exactly what Alhassan did for
the rest of his life.

00:05:14.599 --> 00:05:19.050
In 1940, Alhassan would actually attempt to
open his exporting company.

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:23.319
This way, he didn’t have to rely on the
Royal Niger Company which was now known as

00:05:23.319 --> 00:05:25.080
the United Africa Company.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:29.960
However, his application to become an exporter
would be declined and Alhassan would be forced

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:33.220
to continue working as a middleman for European
companies.

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:37.540
This wasn’t a bad gig by any means though,
so Alhassan just focused on growing what he

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:43.240
had, and by the time he died in 1955, he would
grow to be the richest man in all of West

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:44.240
Africa.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:47.190
It’s crazy to think that he started off
begging for food.

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:51.789
Anyway, moving forward, we have Alhassan’s
children and grandchildren.

00:05:51.789 --> 00:05:56.580
Alhassan had raised his kids and grandchildren
with great appreciation for wealth, hard work,

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:57.580
and success.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:01.400
So, they didn’t just go out and burn all
of Alhassan’s wealth.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:05.030
In fact, they actually did a great job in
maintaining the business and expanding into

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:07.780
more sectors including rice and oats.

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:12.250
With that being said though, none of them
were that ambitious, and they didn’t do

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:14.580
much to take the business to the next level.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:16.949
That is until Aliko Dangote.

00:06:16.949 --> 00:06:21.180
Aliko Dangote was born in Kano on April 10,
1957.

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:25.550
Given that Alhassan had passed away 2 years
before that, Alhassan’s son or Aliko’s

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:28.820
grandfather Sanusi Dantata was running the
business.

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:33.650
Aliko’s father actually passed away early
on, so Aliko would primarily be raised by

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:35.539
his grandfather Sanusi.

00:06:35.539 --> 00:06:39.720
This gave Aliko a unique view into the daily
operations of the business and made him really

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:41.960
interested in growing his own business.

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:46.270
Aliko would attend Al-Azhar university which
is widely known as being the most prestigious

00:06:46.270 --> 00:06:48.080
university in the Islamic world.

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:53.479
Aliko earned a bachelors in business in 1977,
and he would return to Nigeria with a business

00:06:53.479 --> 00:06:55.310
idea ready to go.

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:59.560
His plan was to follow his great grandfather’s
footsteps and expand to soft commodities.

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:03.659
Instead of selling the commodities to Europeans
though, Aliko wanted to import commodities

00:07:03.659 --> 00:07:06.520
into Africa and sell them to local consumers.

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:10.289
His first two targets were rice from Thailand
and sugar from Brazil.

00:07:10.289 --> 00:07:13.889
And to get his business started up, he borrowed
some money from his family.

00:07:13.889 --> 00:07:17.189
Some reports suggest that he borrowed the
money from his uncle while others suggest

00:07:17.189 --> 00:07:19.480
that the money was from his grandfather.

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:24.210
But either way, he got a $2500 loan from one
of his family members and he promised to pay

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:26.050
them back within three years.

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:31.280
Now, that’s no small loan of a million dollars,
but adjusting for inflation, it’s equivalent

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:33.430
to $11,200 today.

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:35.660
So not a small amount per se.

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:39.510
Given his family’s reputation, it wasn’t
too difficult for Aliko to secure some international

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:42.159
suppliers and start selling rice and sugar
locally.

00:07:42.159 --> 00:07:48.000
According to Aliko, the business was a cash
cow, and he profited as much as $10,000 per

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.000
day.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:51.650
That’s the same as $44,800 today.

00:07:51.650 --> 00:07:56.210
Aliko paid back his family member within 3
months, and he was off to the races.

00:07:56.210 --> 00:08:00.639
Over the next two decades, Aliko expanded
from just selling rice and sugar to also selling

00:08:00.639 --> 00:08:02.580
pasta, salt and flour.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:06.610
By expanding his reach in Africa and his line
of products, Aliko grew his personal fortune

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:08.389
to hundreds of millions of dollars.

00:08:08.389 --> 00:08:11.880
But, he wouldn't reach the elusive billion
dollar mark till he embarked on his great

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:13.860
grandfather’s final vision.

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:19.479
Up until 1997, Aliko was simply a middleman
funneling commodities into Africa.

00:08:19.479 --> 00:08:24.500
But in 1997, Aliko decided to venture into
production and stop being the middleman.

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:29.410
He already had a large customer base and plenty
of money though, so the transition wouldn’t

00:08:29.410 --> 00:08:34.520
be too difficult and Aliko would quickly grow
to be one of the only billionaires in Nigeria.

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:38.470
At this point, the Nigerian government would
actually trust him enough to award him a cement

00:08:38.470 --> 00:08:39.600
factory.

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:44.590
To this day, we don’t really know the details
behind why they awarded him the factory.

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:49.431
Maybe the factory was failing and they wanted
him to stage a recovery or maybe it was a

00:08:49.431 --> 00:08:51.450
thank you gift for driving down sugar prices.

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:56.990
I’m not sure, but whatever the reason, Aliko
was awarded a cement factory and he would

00:08:56.990 --> 00:09:00.780
leverage the factory to expand into cement
production as well.

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:04.730
According to Aliko, the key to his success
has been his tendency to reinvest the majority

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:07.330
of his profits back into business.

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:11.330
Apparently, most Afrcan businessmen like to
keep most of their money in cash for some

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:12.330
reason.

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:16.560
All of this reinvesting seems to have worked
out for Aliko as all of his businesses boast

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:18.850
extremely impressive stats today.

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:22.700
The Dangote sugar refinery, for instance,
is the third largest in the world producing

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:26.370
a whopping 800,000 tonnes of sugar annually.

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:30.070
Considering this, it’s not surprising that
Aliko Dangote has been Africa’s richest

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:32.420
person since 2012.

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:36.280
Since reaching the pinnacle of success in
the business world, Aliko has shifted to focusing

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:38.610
on advancing Nigeria as a whole.

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:44.400
In 2009, for instance, Aliko focused on providing
fast internet service to Nigerians by installing

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:47.180
14,000 kilometers of fibre optic.

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:51.710
His latest project is to reduce and potentially
even eliminate Nigeria’s dependence on oil

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:52.710
imports.

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:58.020
Currently, Nigeria imports $7 billion worth
of fuel every year despite the fact that Nigeria

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:00.580
is the biggest oil producer in Africa.

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:06.090
Aliko is investing $15 billion, which is more
than his net worth, into building one of the

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:08.970
world’s biggest oil refineries in Africa.

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:13.980
Hopefully, the oil refinery does come to fruition
and help Nigeria become more oil independent.

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:19.150
But, that’s what Aliko Dangote is working
on today and how he became the richest person

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:20.480
in Africa.

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:23.900
What do you guys think about the story of
Aliko and especially his great grandfather

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:25.200
Alhassan?

